<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">New Zealand National Party</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1936">1936</a>–</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-party-south-africa">National Party</a> is a centre-right party formed in 1936 by a merger of the United and Reform parties in opposition to the first Labour government. It has been one of New Zealand's two main parties ever since, governing under prime ministers including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidney-holland">Sidney Holland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keith-holyoake">Keith Holyoake</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-muldoon">Robert Muldoon</a> and John Key.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The party was formed in May 1936 by a merger of the United Party and the Reform Party. The United Party, called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberal-party-uk">Liberal Party</a> until 1927, drew support from the cities, businesses and middle-class voters. The Reform Party had a rural base and strong support from farmers, then a large share of the population. Like other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> parties, National consists of two bodies: the membership organisation outside parliament and the Parliamentary National Party of elected members.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidney-holland">Sidney Holland</a> became the first National prime minister after the 1949 general election and held office until 1957. His government kept compulsory unionism and the welfare state set up by Labour. During the 151-day Waterfront Dispute of 1951 it acted against the maritime unions, and in the snap election that followed it won 54 of 80 seats. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keith-holyoake">Keith Holyoake</a> succeeded him, lost in 1957 to Labour under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-nash">Walter Nash</a>, and returned in 1960, winning again in 1963, 1966 and 1969 before retiring in 1972.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-muldoon">Robert Muldoon</a> led the party back to power in 1975 with interventionist economic policies, including Bill Birch's Think Big investments in energy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jim-bolger">Jim Bolger</a> won a landslide in 1990 and continued the free-market reforms known as Rogernomics. Jenny Shipley became the first woman prime minister in 1997, and a Labour-led coalition defeated her in 1999. In 2002 National took only 27 of 120 seats, its worst result, but it returned under John Key on 8 November 2008, winning 58 seats, and governed until 2017. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-luxon">Christopher Luxon</a> led it to victory on 14 October 2023, with 38.08% of the party vote and 48 seats.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>National has been described as a broad church of conservative, liberal, populist and libertarian tendencies, united in its early years by opposition to socialism. Its principles include recognition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-waitangi">Treaty of Waitangi</a> as the founding document of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>, limited government and competitive enterprise. It has spent more time in government than any other New Zealand party.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “New Zealand National Party” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Long periods of <b>National Party</b> rule under Sidney Holland and Keith Holyoake alternated with Labour governments under Walter Nash and Norman Kirk.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-zealand">New Zealand · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
